 ## Overview
-Frequency Annealing is a training regularization strategy that gradually activates higher-frequency bands of Fourier Positional Encoding [1267-fourier-positional-encoding.md] during optimization [S1][S3, unofficial][S4]. It solves the problem of optimization instability and local minima caused by high-frequency details appearing too early, which leads to artifacts like self-occlusions or incorrect geometry [S1, S4][S6, unofficial]. Unlike standard Fourier Feature Networks [2765-fourier-feature-networks.md] which use all frequencies from the start, Frequency Annealing employs a coarse-to-fine schedule, effectively narrowing the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) bandwidth initially to prioritize low-frequency structure before refining high-frequency details [S2][S6, unofficial].
+Frequency Annealing is a training regularization strategy that gradually activates higher-frequency bands of Fourier Positional Encoding [1267-fourier-positional-encoding.md] during optimization [S1][S3, unofficial][S4]. It solves the problem of optimization instability and local minima caused by high-frequency details appearing too early, which leads to artifacts like self-occlusions or incorrect geometry [S1, S4][S6, unofficial]. Unlike standard Fourier Feature Networks [2765-fourier-feature-networks.md] which use all frequencies from the start, Frequency Annealing employs a coarse-to-fine schedule, effectively narrowing the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) bandwidth initially to prioritize low-frequency structure before refining high-frequency details [S9][S6, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-Frequency Annealing modifies the input to the MLP by applying a time-dependent window function to the frequency bands of the positional encoding. This ensures that low-frequency components dominate the gradient updates early in training, while high-frequency components are gradually introduced.
